## Does this plan make sense for your job hunt?

Yes, more than the AZ-104/AZ-500/SC-500 stack you linked. Reasons:

- **SC-300 is your highest-leverage cert.** It maps 1:1 to your resume (Entra ID, Conditional Access, JML, identity governance) and to the remote roles you’re targeting. Salary data backs it: \$115K+ for IAM specialists, 473 active US postings.[^46_1][^46_2][^46_3][^46_4]
- **"Support any customer across any industry" is the right ambition.** That’s exactly what senior consultants/architects do — and SC-300’s domains (user identities, auth, workload identities, governance) are the same in healthcare, finance, retail, SaaS.[^46_5][^46_6]
- **The gap is not exam knowledge; it’s industry translation.** Most people can configure Conditional Access. Few can explain *why* a bank needs phishing-resistant MFA but a startup can start with number matching, or how DPDPA changes access-review cadence in India vs. GDPR in Germany.[^46_7]

**One warning:** Don’t try to prep AZ-104 + AZ-500 + SC-300 + SC-500 simultaneously. That’s a 6-month path, not 10 days. Park the others. SC-300 alone gets you the interviews you want.[^46_8]

## Custom SC-300 Mastery Map (exam + what the exam misses)

This is the structure I’ll use for the new repo. It’s built from the official SC-300 study guide, then expanded with the deep-dive topics the exam only touches lightly.[^46_6][^46_9][^46_5]

### Domain 1: Implement and manage user identities (20–25%)

**Exam scope:** users, groups, hybrid identity (Connect, cloud sync, PHS/PTA), external identities (B2B, social IdPs), administrative units, custom domains.
**Deep-dive additions (not tested in depth):**

- ImmutableID / source anchor choices (ms-DS-ConsistencyGuid vs objectGUID) and when to change them in a merger/acquisition.
- Tenant-to-tenant migration patterns (M\&A, divestiture) — cross-tenant sync, staged cutover, coexistence.
- Hybrid identity failure modes: PHS vs PTA vs federation decision tree, TLS/cert dependencies, Connect Health alerting, staging mode swing migration.
- External identity lifecycle: B2B invitation redemption, Google/social IdP claims mapping, guest access reviews, cross-tenant access settings.


### Domain 2: Implement authentication and access management (25–30%)

**Exam scope:** MFA (all methods), Conditional Access, Identity Protection, passwordless (FIDO2, Windows Hello), session controls, authentication contexts.
**Deep-dive additions:**

- Phishing-resistant MFA vs. "any MFA" — why FIDO2/passkeys matter for finance/healthcare, AAGUIDs, attestation.
- Conditional Access architecture: policy evaluation order, report-only mode, break-glass accounts, CA policy-as-code with Graph API, backup/restore.
- Identity Protection risk policies vs. CA policies — when to use which, user risk vs. sign-in risk, investigation workbook.
- Legacy auth blocking strategy (SMTP, IMAP, POP, older Office clients) and the exception-handling pattern.


### Domain 3: Plan and implement workload identities (20–25%)

**Exam scope:** app registrations, enterprise apps, managed identities, service principals, OAuth 2.0 / OIDC / SAML SSO, SCIM provisioning.
**Deep-dive additions:**

- App registration vs. enterprise app — the security boundary and why "App Owner" is a critical role.
- OAuth 2.0 flows in production: auth code + PKCE for SPAs, client credentials for daemons, on-behalf-of for APIs, device code for kiosks.
- Managed identities vs. service principals vs. workload identity federation — which one for GitHub Actions, AKS pods, Azure Functions, third-party SaaS.
- SCIM provisioning failure modes: attribute mapping, soft-delete behavior, re-provisioning loops, source-anchor mismatches.


### Domain 4: Plan and automate identity governance (20–25%)

**Exam scope:** PIM, access reviews, entitlement management, lifecycle workflows, terms of use.
**Deep-dive additions:**

- PIM for groups vs. PIM for roles vs. PIM for Azure resources — activation approval workflows, justification/audit trail, MFA on activation.
- Access review design: recurring vs. one-time, reviewee selection, group owners vs. app owners, auto-apply vs. manual remediation.
- Entitlement management: access packages, catalog scoping, connected organizations, sponsor/approver chains, external user lifecycle.
- Lifecycle workflows (JML automation): joiner/mover/leaver task logic, custom task extensions with Azure Logic Apps, and how this maps to your real PowerShell/Graph work.
- Compliance mapping: how governance controls satisfy SOC 2 CC6, ISO 27001 A.5.15–A.5.18, DPDPA access-rights clauses.


### Cross-cutting industry use cases (the "any customer" layer)

Each domain will include a short **industry pattern** section:

- **Finance/FinTech:** SOX ITGC quarterly UARs, PCI 7/8 access scoping, phishing-resistant MFA for payment systems, JIT admin for trading platforms.
- **Healthcare:** HIPAA minimum-necessary access, clinician fast user switching, emergency access accounts, BAA constraints on guest sharing.
- **Retail/e-commerce:** seasonal worker JML at scale, POS device compliance, B2C customer identity (CIAM) basics vs. B2E workforce identity.
- **SaaS/startup:** rapid M365 onboarding, SOC 2 evidence automation, lean team break-glass patterns, cost-conscious license management.
- **Manufacturing/OT:** legacy protocol coexistence, shared device accounts, network-segmented Conditional Access, supplier B2B.
- **Education/nonprofit:** student lifecycle (enrollment → graduation), volunteer access, donor PII in SharePoint, grant-driven access reviews.


## 10-day high-focus curriculum (8–10 hrs/day)

I’ll build this as a new public repo: `patelkeveen/sc300-mastery` (or whatever name you prefer). Each day = one folder with:

- `README.md` — concept deep-dive + Decision Stack framing
- `labs/` — hands-on steps in a free M365 E5 developer tenant + Azure free tier
- `use-cases/` — industry-specific scenarios with implementation notes
- `tickets.md` — common misconfigurations and troubleshooting
- `interview-notes.md` — how to explain this to a customer/architect

| Day | Focus | Deliverable |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| 1 | Repo setup + Domain 1 core (users, groups, hybrid identity) | Tenant baseline + hybrid sync lab |
| 2 | Domain 1 deep-dive (external identities, tenant migration) | B2B lab + migration runbook |
| 3 | Domain 2 core (MFA, Conditional Access) | CA policy matrix + break-glass lab |
| 4 | Domain 2 deep-dive (Identity Protection, passwordless, session controls) | Risk-based CA lab + FIDO2 setup |
| 5 | Domain 3 core (app registrations, enterprise apps, SSO) | SAML + OIDC app integration lab |
| 6 | Domain 3 deep-dive (managed identities, workload identity federation, SCIM) | GitHub Actions → Azure with WIF + SCIM provisioning lab |
| 7 | Domain 4 core (PIM, access reviews) | PIM activation + review lab |
| 8 | Domain 4 deep-dive (entitlement mgmt, lifecycle workflows, compliance mapping) | Access package + JML workflow lab |
| 9 | Industry use-case synthesis (finance, healthcare, SaaS, retail, manufacturing) | 5 industry implementation guides |
| 10 | Mock exam + weak-point remediation + customer-facing explanations | SC-300 readiness report + teach-back videos/notes |

**Daily loop (same as your repo method):** Learn → Lab → Break it → Explain (child → SME) → Industry use case → Ticket/log.

