# @GitHub It could be well more indepth and enhanced with missing things

Yes. The earlier `azure-security-mastery` idea was still too close to a certification roadmap. The stronger version should be an **Azure engineering and customer-delivery system**: certifications provide the coverage baseline, while labs, incidents, architecture decisions, automation, and industry scenarios prove that you can operate real environments.

Microsoft’s AZ-104 scope already spans identity/governance, storage, compute, networking, monitoring, and recovery—not merely Entra ID. The new repository should therefore connect AZ-104, AZ-500, SC-300, SC-500/Cloud \& AI Security, AI, NHI, networking, and operational engineering into one coherent system.[^49_1]

## Better repository identity

Recommended name:

```text
azure-cloud-security-mastery
```

Why this is better than `entra-id-mastery`:

- It does not trap you in an IAM-admin identity.
- It supports Azure administrator, cloud security, identity security, platform security, AI security, and consulting roles.
- It gives networking, compute, storage, governance, and operations equal importance.
- It allows Microsoft certifications to be treated as **curriculum inputs**, not as the repository’s purpose.

The repository’s public positioning should be:

> A hands-on Azure cloud, security, identity, networking, and AI engineering curriculum designed around real customer problems, operational failure, automation, governance, and explainable architecture.

## The missing layers

The repo needs more than “fundamentals, intermediate, mastery.” It needs **seven dimensions of mastery** for every major capability:


| Dimension | Question it must answer |
| :-- | :-- |
| Fundamentals | What is this and why does it exist? |
| Implementation | Can I create and configure it? |
| Integration | Can I connect it to identity, networking, data, and applications? |
| Operations | Can I monitor, troubleshoot, patch, scale, and recover it? |
| Security | Can I harden it and prove least privilege? |
| Architecture | Can I select it against alternatives and defend trade-offs? |
| Customer delivery | Can I discover requirements, implement safely, document, train, and hand over the solution? |

A topic is not “mastered” because a lab succeeds once. It becomes mastery-level only when you can deploy it, break it, diagnose it, secure it, automate it, explain its trade-offs, and apply it to multiple customer environments.

